<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">District of Pilten</h1><p class="st-meta">1585 CE – 1795 CE · capital Pilten</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Baltic territory around Piltene, the remnant of the Bishopric of Courland, governed by its own nobles under the Polish crown until 1795.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-duke-of-holstein">Magnus of Holstein</a> died in 1583, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sold its rights to the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a> to Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1585. The district, scattered around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piltene">Pilten</a> (Piltene) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizpute">Hasenpoth</a> in western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a>, was not merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a> but held directly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The local German nobility governed through their own diet and kept wide privileges, with a Lutheran church. The dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> repeatedly claimed the district and controlled it for periods, while the nobles resisted, preferring the distant Polish king. Latvian peasants worked the estates as serfs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piltene">Pilten</a> was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-courland">Governorate of Courland</a>. Its separate status disappeared, though its nobility kept privileges as part of the Courland knighthood.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/district-of-pilten.jpg" alt="District of Pilten" width="320" height="328"><figcaption>Image: Athanasius Soter at et.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kura_pk.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1585 CE – 1795 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pilten</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latvian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Piltene, Stift Pilten, Bishopric of Pilten</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1690&amp;focus=district-of-pilten" aria-label="District of Pilten on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5147 251 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where District of Pilten lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5467 507L5467 494M5467 507L5457 693M5467 507L5513 509M5467 507L5513 509"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5467 494h0M5457 693h0M5513 509h0M5513 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5467 507h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5467 507h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1690&amp;focus=district-of-pilten">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
